The `Colors` static class already contains the standardized colour names as properties.
Here is an implementation in C# using reflection:

```cs
public static class ColorExtensions {
    public static FrozenDictionary<string, Color> NamedColors { get; } = typeof(Colors).GetProperties()
        .Where(NamedColorProperty => NamedColorProperty.PropertyType == typeof(Color))
        .ToFrozenDictionary(NamedColorProperty => NamedColorProperty.Name, NamedColorProperty => (Color)NamedColorProperty.GetValue(null)!);

    public static KeyValuePair<string, Color> ToNamedColor(this Color Color) {
        return NamedColors.MinBy(NamedColor => NamedColor.Value.DistanceTo(Color));
    }
    public static float DistanceTo(this Color Color, Color OtherColor) {
        Vector4 ColorVector = new(Color.R, Color.G, Color.B, Color.A);
        Vector4 OtherColorVector = new(OtherColor.R, OtherColor.G, OtherColor.B, OtherColor.A);
        return ColorVector.DistanceTo(OtherColorVector);
    }
}
```

This returns the colour name in PascalCase (e.g. AliceBlue).
